# box.view: a framed block with a TITLE, a label column down the left and the text beside
# it, separated by a gutter bar. The one view that draws a border.
#
# The way in is a decorator over a plain markdown table, which is the whole point: where
# this hook does not run, the reader still gets a real table.
#
#   @{view:box}
#   | TL;DR | |
#   | --- | --- |
#   | SAID | - the flaky retry test is fixed, root cause was a shared fixture |
#   | DID | - retries no longer mutate the shared fixture, 3 suites back to green |
#   | TRACE | - Read `retry.ts`, `queue.test.ts` |
#   | | - Ran `pnpm test` (2x) |
#   | NEXT | - bump the patch version, or wait for the queue refactor? |
#
# The HEADER ROW is the frame, and that is the whole difference with columns.view and
# lines.view, which read it as the names of their columns. Here its first cell is the
# TITLE, drawn inside the border above a full-width rule, and its last cell is a BADGE set
# into the top rule near the right corner. Anything between the two is not read: a box has
# two chrome slots and no more.
#
# So the header is never blank here. `| | |`, the form markdown forces on a table wanting
# no header, draws an empty title row and its rule all the same: this view is a box, and a
# box with nothing above the rule is a table with a border. Want no title, use lines.view.
#
# Every row is SET OFF by a blank line, which is what makes the block read as a summary
# rather than as a table with a border. The blank is emitted per row: the container's
# blank-run collapsing is what keeps it to one and drops the one trailing the last row, so
# a separator appears only BETWEEN two rows that printed.
#
# Never between a row and its CONTINUATION, though: an empty label cell continues the
# section above, and the loop drops a separator written before one, the blank exactly as it
# drops an `@rule`. So several lines under one label read as one section, and a table whose
# rows carry no label at all is one section too, drawn dense.
#
# That wrap is the other half: a long line folds inside the border, and the fold keeps the
# bar and blanks the label, so it stays in its column instead of restarting at the margin.
# `bullet=""` is what buys that boundary, at `${#bullet}`, and prints nothing.
#
# The tone slot colours the LABEL and the BAR together and nothing else, so a box is one
# colour and never a rainbow:
#
#   @{view:box}                 the default below, grey
#   @{view:box, tone:cyan}      labels and bars cyan, the text still white
#   @{view:box, type:warning}   yellow, and the message still says what it IS
#
# The bar is U+258E, the one wrap.ts calls GUTTER_BAR, and it is not decoration: it is the
# glyph a continuation row is REDRAWN with, so it has to be the same one. Consecutive rows
# join it into a single frange down the block.
#
# It is written FIRST, right after the label, which is what it sets off from the rest, and
# the separators past it are the thin one the two other table views draw. That the first is
# the one always left standing is the hollow rule's doing (see below), so a two-column
# table, which is what this view is for, keeps the bar and drops the two thin ones.
#
# The bullet is the AUTHOR's, written in the cell, never furniture this file adds: `- ` on
# a note and `□ R1 ·` on a reminder are the same column, and a template drawing its own
# would impose one on the section that wanted the other. The content cell is the LAST
# field, emitted verbatim, so it carries whatever emphasis the message wrote.
#
# Two to four columns, like the two other table views and for the same reason: the carrier
# hands this view whatever the table had, and a middle column it did not declare would be
# dropped in silence. The middles sit past the bar, between the label and the text, and a
# column no row carries takes the furniture written AFTER it down with it (the hollow rule,
# src/layout/columns.ts), so a two-column table draws exactly a label, a bar and a line.
#
# cap="1/3" keeps a long label from pushing the text to the margin: past a third of the
# width it FOLDS, one row per line, in step with the text beside it. It costs the row
# height and never a character, an ellipsis being a piece of the data the reader cannot
# get back.
@tone dim
@fields rows label mid1 mid2 content
@box
@head {{box_title}}${head.label}{{/}}
@right ${head.content}
@each rows cap="1/3" bullet=""
{{b}}{{tone}}${label}{{/}}  {{tone}}▎{{/}} ${mid1}  {{tone}}│{{/}}  ${mid2}  {{tone}}│{{/}}  ${#bullet}${content}

@end
@endbox
